<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abkhaz people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Abkhazians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abkhaz are a people of the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> who live mainly in Abkhazia, on the eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. They speak Abkhaz, a Northwest Caucasian language. Ruled by their own princes under Georgian and later Ottoman influence, they came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the 19th century, and many were deported or emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the 1860s and 1870s. Abkhazia broke away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in the war of 1992 and 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abkhaz-people.jpg" alt="Abkhaz people" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apsuwara · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abkhaz-deputatklk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Some scholars say the ancient Heniochi tribe were the progenitors of the Abkhaz. This warlike people came into contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> through the Greek colonies of Dioskourias and Pitiuntas. In the Roman period the Abasgoi, or Abasgi, are mentioned as inhabiting the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> described them as warlike worshippers of three deities.</p><p>The Abasgi lived within the Lazian Empire, also called Egrisi, a state in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Their monarch was appointed by the Laz king with the approval of the Byzantine emperor. After Egrisi declined, the Abasgian prince Leon I acquired it with Byzantine support in the 7th century, and Abasgia became a kingdom with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>. It soon adopted Georgian in place of Greek, and in 978 Abkhazia united with the eastern Georgian kingdom through dynastic succession, forming the Kingdom of Georgia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> became the dominant religion of the Abkhaz in the 6th century, under the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>, and was followed under the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in the High Middle Ages. The region came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the 16th century, which introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and the population gradually became Muslim in the 16th and 17th centuries.</p><p>The Russian conquest, from the 1810s to the 1860s, was accompanied by a mass expulsion of Muslim Abkhaz to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>; Christian Abkhaz were largely spared. A strong policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> followed, and Russian replaced Georgian as the second language of the Abkhaz, which alienated them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> despite their common heritage. As a result the Abkhaz diaspora is estimated at no less than twice the number living in Abkhazia. Some Abkhaz fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War</a> of 1919 to 1922, and thousands then fled persecution to Greece. The war in Abkhazia of 1992 and 1993 left the Abkhaz an ethnic plurality of about 45 percent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Abkhaz language belongs to the Northwest Caucasian family, also called Abkhaz–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a> or Pontic, and the Abkhaz are closely related to Circassians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abazins">Abazins</a>. There are three subgroups: the Bzyb, living by the Bzyb River; the Abzhui, living by the Kodori River, whose dialect is the basis of the literary language; and the Samurzakan in the southeast. The traditional economy rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> breeding, beekeeping, viticulture and agriculture.</p><p>The Abkhaz are divided mainly between Orthodox Christian and Sunni Muslim of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a>, prevalent in Abkhazia and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> respectively, though indigenous non-Abrahamic beliefs have always been strong. Many Abkhaz villages, some 250 Abkhaz-Abaza ones by one estimate, lie in Turkey, and the community there is believed to be larger than that in Abkhazia. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> the Abaza family, which originated in Abkhazia, has contributed to political and literary life since the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abkhazians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic was led by the popular <b>Abkhaz</b> Bolshevik Nestor Lakoba, who slowed collectivisation and protected local landowners and traditions.</blockquote><a href="/state/abkhaz-ssr">Abkhaz SSR</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Kingdom of Georgia broke up in the 1460s, the Shervashidze, or Chachba, family who governed the <b>Abkhaz</b> coast as eristavis under the Dadiani of Mingrelia made themselves independent princes with their seat at Sukhumi and later Lykhny.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-abkhazia">Principality of Abkhazia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ubykhs lived on the Black Sea coast and mountain valleys around modern Sochi, between the Shapsugs and the <b>Abkhaz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ubykhia">Ubykhia</a></li></ul></section>
